exercicios.explicacao_md:
Forms are the front door for user data. You read what was typed with `.value` (text fields) and `.checked` (checkboxes), and you **validate** it before accepting it. On submit, the browser tries to reload the page — `e.preventDefault()` stops that so your JavaScript can take control.

```js
form.addEventListener("submit", function (e) {
  e.preventDefault();
  var name = field.value;
  if (name.trim() === "") {
    message.textContent = "Please fill in your name!";
  } else {
    message.textContent = "Hello, " + name;
  }
});
```

Notice the `.trim()`: it strips spaces from both ends, so a field with **only spaces** also counts as empty. Without validation, bad data gets into the system.

