exercicios.explicacao_md:
Interactive components (modals, tabs, menus) keep a **state**: open or closed, active or inactive. In the browser, the most common way to represent that state is a **CSS class** on the element. JavaScript adds or removes the class, and CSS decides how each state looks.

```js
var panel = document.getElementById("panel");
// show:
panel.classList.remove("hidden");
// hide:
panel.classList.add("hidden");
// toggle (on/off):
panel.classList.toggle("hidden");
```

With `.hidden { display: none }` in the CSS, adding the class hides the element and removing it shows it. Opening and closing become just two operations on the same class. The preview on the right updates live — click and watch the state change.

